{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-65931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is any integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-65931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-65931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-65931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-65931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{e}^{x}+5?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/f5983db4731c3fde0899ffc7afd602c7c82502f581742b62e69e8e6cf2d99e93.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{e}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/844a32e9d3640f9244b867c7411241171902c848defa0bf653b3820c3850238d.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $5$ units up to give $y=-{e}^{x}+5.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c4cd8597702179629617890fce31272aa447a3227b74231e787a61eb4e770589.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/01cc790392c233d00b4ab30e939230a0e7ac31708a0f568557a3e36e2cf04d67.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/28e24035715baa30e89392408359033dc402264c0fdcdc5968addaa615aedf81.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/2ebda1ba75caec9c62a9758696d2c4c45b452c29088e3811edfacd03acf3cd70.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b83ad0f69e6d4a61227c77b3dede3632accdceaa59c1bf63da8ca8bc3fe5a7f9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/be39498e5ad2c3148b254844722af707d721d7447a393a2341c6c0f6690c603e.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-28595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b83ad0f69e6d4a61227c77b3dede3632accdceaa59c1bf63da8ca8bc3fe5a7f9.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/3303e36eb359692802a1c2184a2c54591f0b5de05853691841191a65689e8735.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of an object. Calculate the deceleration of the object." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The object is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(30,10)$ and $(50,5),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}-10\\text{ m/s}}{50\\text{ s}-30\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}}{20\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =-0.25\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the object is $-0.25\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ which means that the deceleration of the object is $0.25\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "0.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-59454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},$ $\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,…,\\,55,\\,60,\\,65,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,…,\\,54,\\,60,\\,66,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-5)^{5},(x-5)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y-2)^{3},(y-2)^{3},$ and $(y-2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2})=(x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot (x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "30(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}"] ["math" "60(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}"] ["math" "60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}"] ["math" "90(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}"] ["math" "90(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-112230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b0b100942a658966a5feea8a8677e7cfe86f21970a443385ff06034bbcb3d497.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ACD$ and $△ABC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/7e3232258a523e4dbfebb6c380f5b88c2a54036b4d719e70ebf9060054f825d7.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ACD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =189\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈163.679,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△ABC:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & =540\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈294.105,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ACD}+{A}_{ABC} \\\\  & =163.679+294.105 \\\\  & =457.784\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $458.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "416"] ["math" "422"] ["math" "437"] ["math" "458"] ["math" "462"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-67788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "458"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Rewrite $3{q}^{2}-9q+15$ by completing the square." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{(q-3)}^{2}+3"] ["math" "3{(q-3)}^{2}-12"] ["math" "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}"] ["math" "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-35081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $n\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)n!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $n!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}} & =\\frac{n!\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{n!}\\cdot \\cancel{n!}}{(n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{n+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{n+1}{{n}^{2}+2}"] ["math" "n+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-19847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-340099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function  $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-340099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2+e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-340099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-340099" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-340099" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $4+\\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2(n+1)}{n}"] ["math" "\\frac{4(n+1)}{n}"] ["math" "\\frac{4n+1}{4n}"] ["math" "\\frac{n+4}{4n}"] ["math" "\\frac{n+4}{n}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-41574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4(n+1)}{n}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-3,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-3,1).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-3,1].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $h(x)$, $p(x)$, and $q(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-3,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/198f84781bcafa5560244ccf6e87df23a8d3e0ab621aaedb3c17df3fe1e52e31.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/4df80bb9377923dfd59a686761c6dc3bd7517213f4da3f0e39401215ea22dbdd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/6d66f5a7373826c316010328eaab1d6ac4cf64a82f9c7038b1f6f9450efd3388.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/7c1114ff20331e5a1100523a460b8dddb017ea54389deb5f9388d16da4742d9e.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-105396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43b585ddd5aee5595c3b4eef72880c6c6ad5cb0b4fd5209e9803447579eb7e89"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"} {"question" "q-117799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{9}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/state.json" "file_sha256" "5301e3aa78dc5a619ddd7ffb1e27676177ad58f5c12f11910f616839449d5dd6"}] "usage" {"q-41574" [] "q-65931" []} "basis" 1122 "retractions" [[17592186180119 :question/problem "Write $4 + \\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction."] [17592186204559 :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y = 3\\sin (2x + \\frac{π}{2})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186178590 17592186178591 17592186178592 1759218